  "worked_solution": "The particle's velocity vector is given by the derivative of its position vector:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v & =\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t} \\\\  & =\u27e8\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t},\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\u27e9 \\\\  & =\u27e8\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t\\operatorname{cos}\u2061t),\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t+\\operatorname{sin}\u2061t)\u27e9 \\\\  & =\u27e8\\operatorname{cos}\u2061t-t\\operatorname{sin}\u2061t,1+\\operatorname{cos}\u2061t\u27e9\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the velocity when $t=\\frac{\\pi }{2},$ we substitute into the above:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(\\frac{\\pi }{2}) & =\u27e8\\operatorname{cos}\u2061(\\frac{\\pi }{2})-(\\frac{\\pi }{2})\\operatorname{sin}\u2061(\\frac{\\pi }{2}),1+\\operatorname{cos}\u2061(\\frac{\\pi }{2})\u27e9 \\\\  & =\u27e80-(\\frac{\\pi }{2})(1),1+(0)\u27e9 \\\\  & =\u27e8-\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\,1\u27e9\\end{aligned}\n$$",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
